# Rule: Tailwind CSS Class Order

## Description
Establishes a consistent ordering convention for Tailwind CSS classes to improve readability and maintainability of React components.

## Benefits
- Improves code scanability and readability
- Makes class conflicts easier to spot
- Facilitates easier collaboration on components
- Reduces merge conflicts from reordering classes
- Makes code reviews more efficient

## Recommended Class Order

1. Layout & Display (`flex`, `grid`, `block`, `hidden`, etc.)
2. Positioning (`static`, `absolute`, `relative`, etc.)
3. Box Model (`w-`, `h-`, `p-`, `m-`, etc.)
4. Flexbox/Grid (`flex-`, `grid-`, `gap-`, etc.)
5. Typography (`text-`, `font-`, etc.)
6. Visual Styling (`bg-`, `border-`, `shadow-`, etc.)
7. Interactions (`hover:`, `focus:`, etc.)
8. Responsive Variants (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, etc.)

## Bad Examples

```tsx
// ❌ Random order of classes making it hard to scan
function Card() {
  return (
    <div className="rounded-lg hover:shadow-lg text-gray-800 p-4 bg-white flex mb-4">
      <h2 className="font-bold text-xl border-b pb-2 text-blue-600">Card Title</h2>
      <p className="mt-2 text-sm">Card content goes here</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// ❌ Mixing responsive variants throughout instead of grouping them
function ResponsiveButton() {
  return (
    <button className="bg-blue-500 md:bg-blue-600 px-4 md:px-6 py-2 md:py-3 text-white rounded">
      Submit
    </button>
  );
}
```

## Good Examples

```tsx
// ✅ Classes organized by category
function Card() {
  return (
    <div className="flex mb-4 p-4 bg-white rounded-lg text-gray-800 hover:shadow-lg">
      <h2 className="pb-2 font-bold text-xl text-blue-600 border-b">Card Title</h2>
      <p className="mt-2 text-sm">Card content goes here</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ Responsive variants grouped at the end
function ResponsiveButton() {
  return (
    <button className="px-4 py-2 bg-blue-500 text-white rounded md:px-6 md:py-3 md:bg-blue-600">
      Submit
    </button>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ Using complex arrangement with proper ordering
function FeatureSection() {
  return (
    <section className="
      /* Layout */
      flex flex-col
      /* Box Model */
      w-full max-w-4xl mx-auto p-6
      /* Visual */
      bg-gray-50 rounded-xl shadow-sm
      /* Typography */
      text-gray-800
      /* Responsive */
      sm:p-8 sm:flex-row md:gap-8
    ">
      <div className="flex-1 mb-6 sm:mb-0">
        <h3 className="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Feature Title</h3>
        <p className="text-base">Feature description goes here</p>
      </div>
      <div className="flex-1">
        {/* Content */}
      </div>
    </section>
  );
}
```

## Implementation

For larger className strings (more than ~5 classes), consider using one of these approaches:

1. **Template Literals with Comments**:
```tsx
<div className={`
  /* Layout */
  flex flex-col
  /* Spacing */
  p-4 my-6
  /* Visual */
  bg-white rounded-lg
  /* Responsive */
  md:flex-row md:p-6
`}>
```

2. **Using classnames or clsx library**:
```tsx
import clsx from 'clsx';

<div className={clsx(
  // Layout
  'flex flex-col',
  // Spacing
  'p-4 my-6',
  // Visual
  'bg-white rounded-lg',
  // Responsive
  'md:flex-row md:p-6',
  // Conditional
  isActive && 'border-2 border-blue-500'
)}>
```

3. **Using Tailwind's @apply in separate CSS file** (for frequently reused patterns):
```css
/* styles.css */
.feature-card {
  @apply flex flex-col p-4 my-6 bg-white rounded-lg md:flex-row md:p-6;
}
```

## Exceptions
- For very simple components with few classes, strict ordering may be unnecessary
- When using third-party component libraries that generate their own classes
- For dynamically generated class names that cannot be reordered manually

